# How to Require a Fork with Composer

When you need to use a specific fork of an open-source project that isn't yet packaged on Packagist, Composer forces you to register the repository first. The error `no matching package found` is your immediate signal that Composer cannot locate the package in its standard search paths. This usually happens because the `repositories` section in your `composer.json` is defined incorrectly or lacks the necessary details for Composer to map the package name to a Git URL.

To resolve this, you must add a repository definition that explicitly tells Composer where to fetch the code. In the scenario provided, the user attempts to use Nodge's fork of `lessphp`. The configuration snippet shows a common mistake: defining a "package" type repository without ensuring the version constraint in the `require` section matches the available versions in that specific repository definition.

The source material demonstrates a valid approach using a package-type repository definition, but there is a critical detail regarding how the version is handled. When you define a repository as a "package," you are essentially creating a virtual package within your project's dependency graph. You must ensure that the `version` field inside the `repositories` section aligns with what you specify in the `require` section.

Here is the corrected structure for your `composer.json`. Note the specific placement of the version constraint:

```json
{
    "name": "your-project/your-package",
    "description": "Project description",
    "type": "project",
    "license": "MIT",
    "authors": [
        {
            "name": "Your Name",
            "email": "you@example.com"
        }
    ],
    "require": {
        "php": ">=5.3.3",
        "nodge/lessphp": "dev-master"
    },
    "repositories": [
        {
            "type": "package",
            "package": {
                "name": "nodge/lessphp",
                "description": "Nodge's fork of lessphp",
                "type": "library",
                "version": "dev-master",
                "source": {
                    "url": "https://github.com/Nodge/lessphp.git",
                    "type": "git",
                    "reference": "master"
                },
                "dist": null,
                "autoload": {
                    "classmap": ["lessc.inc.php"]
                }
            }
        }
    ]
}
```

In this configuration, the `version` field inside the `repositories` block is set to `dev-master`. This tells Composer that whenever it resolves the package name `nodge/lessphp` against this virtual repository, it should look for the version `dev-master`. Consequently, your `require` section can safely ask for `"nodge/lessphp": "dev-master"`.

A frequent pitfall occurs when developers omit the `version` field inside the repository definition or mismatch it with the requirement. If you do not specify a version in the package definition, Composer might assume a default or fail to match the specific Git tag or branch you intend to use. Additionally, while the example uses `classmap` for autoloading, this is highly specific to that particular fork. Generally, using `psr-4` autoloaders is preferred if the upstream project supports it, but for forks that rely on legacy files like `lessc.inc.php`, the classmap approach ensures the file is found immediately without needing to modify the autoloader configuration of the main framework.

You might wonder why not just use a VCS repository type instead. While `composer.json` supports `"type": "vcs"` repositories which point directly to a Git URL, the "package" type allows you to define metadata like `description`, `homepage`, and specific autoload rules within the same block as the versioning logic. This is often cleaner for single-package forks where you want to treat the fork exactly as if it were a stable release on Packagist, just hosted differently.

Once the file is saved, running `composer update` will trigger Composer to fetch the repository definition, locate the Git URL, and clone the `master` branch into your `vendor/nodge/lessphp` directory. If you later decide to switch back to the official version on Packagist, you can simply remove the entry from the `repositories` section and change the requirement to `"lessephp": "1.4.0"` (or whatever the latest stable version is), provided the package exists in the main registry.

This method of requiring forks is standard practice when upstream projects lag behind or when a specific fix needs immediate integration without waiting for an official release cycle. It gives you full control over which branch or tag your application depends on, ensuring stability across deployments as long as you pin the version string correctly.
